/**
 * Ready-made example files — the "Examples" menu in the menu bar and
 * the "New example…" context-menu item in the Explorer.
 *
 * Each example is a complete, runnable document created into a chapter
 * folder via POST /api/file-create. Identifiers inside the content and
 * the generated FILE NAME both honor the naming-style settings
 * (Settings → Editor → Naming style), so a camelCase user gets
 * `monteCarloPi.py` with `estimatePi` while a snake_case user gets
 * `monte_carlo_pi.py` with `estimate_pi`.
 *
 * the library lives in `examples-data/` (one module per
 * category) with a SUBCATEGORY so the menus nest language → category →
 * subcategory → examples.
 *
 * the library grew to 1,732 entries — Python expanded to
 * EXACTLY 1,000 entries across deeper subcategories ("1000 py examples
 * with more sub cats"), every Markdown / Text / Other category was
 * topped up to exactly 50 entries ("50 in each cat other"), and FIVE
 * new language groups joined the menu — R, SQL, JSON, YAML and Shell
 * ("add 5 more cats"). The first modules are generated by
 * the example generators and validated by scripts/check-examples.ts.
 *
 * eight more categories (25 entries each) — Async &
 * Concurrency, Regex Cookbook, Web Scraping, Seaborn & Plotly, CLI
 * Applications, SQLite & Databases, Decorators & Metaclasses, and
 * Itertools Recipes — bring the library to ~2,700 entries and Python
 * to exactly 2,000. The next modules are generated by
 * the example generators and validated by scripts/check-examples.ts.
 *
 * six more
 * Python categories (25 entries each) — Type Hints & Protocols,
 * Errors & Exceptions, Testing & Doctests, Logging & Debugging,
 * Dates & Times, and Hashing & Crypto — bring Python to exactly
 * 2,150 and the library to ~2,850 entries. The latest modules are
 * generated by the example generators (each one emits, then the
 * validator compiles + executes every body) and tallied by
 * scripts/check-examples.ts.
 */
